What is Aggregation?
Aggregation means combining multiple rows of data into a single summary row, usually using aggregate functions like:
| Function  | Description            | Example       |
| --------- | ---------------------- | ------------- |
| `COUNT()` | Counts number of rows  | `COUNT(*)`    |
| `SUM()`   | Adds up numeric values | `SUM(salary)` |
| `AVG()`   | Finds average          | `AVG(salary)` |
| `MIN()`   | Finds smallest value   | `MIN(salary)` |
| `MAX()`   | Finds largest value    | `MAX(salary)` |

👉 You use them with GROUP BY when you want summary results per group (like per department, per month, etc.).

Example:
SELECT 
  COUNT(*) AS total_employees,
  SUM(salary) AS total_salary,
  AVG(salary) AS avg_salary
FROM employees;

GROUP BY:
is used in SQL to group rows that have the same values in one or more columns.
Example:
SELECT dept_id,
       COUNT(*) AS emp_count,
       AVG(salary) AS avg_salary
FROM employees
GROUP BY dept_id;

SELECT dept_id, job_title, COUNT(*) AS emp_count
FROM employees
GROUP BY dept_id, job_title;


Filtering Aggregated Results — HAVING
The WHERE clause filters rows before grouping,
but HAVING filters after grouping.

| Clause   | Works On        | Used To Filter  |
| -------- | --------------- | --------------- |
| `WHERE`  | Individual rows | Before grouping |
| `HAVING` | Grouped rows    | After grouping  |

-- WHERE filters rows first
-- HAVING filters aggregated groups
SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS emp_count
FROM employees
WHERE salary > 40000
GROUP BY dept_id
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

| Concept            | Example                     | Purpose               |
| ------------------ | --------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Aggregate all rows | `AVG(salary)`               | Get one summary row   |
| Grouping           | `GROUP BY dept_id`          | Summarize per group   |
| Filtering groups   | `HAVING AVG(salary)>60000`  | Filter after grouping |
| Sort aggregates    | `ORDER BY SUM(salary)`      | Sort summaries        |
| Unique count       | `COUNT(DISTINCT dept_id)`   | Count unique          |
| Group expressions  | `CASE WHEN` inside GROUP BY | Custom grouping       |

GROUP BY vs DISTINCT:
| Feature                               | `DISTINCT`                 | `GROUP BY`                                        |
| ------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Purpose                               | Removes duplicate rows     | Groups rows to perform **aggregate calculations** |
| Can use aggregates (SUM, COUNT, AVG)? | ❌ No                       | ✅ Yes                                          |
| Output                                | Only unique combinations   | One row per group                                 |
| Keyword position                      | Comes right after `SELECT` | Comes after `WHERE`                               |

Correlated vs Non-Correlated Subquery:
Non-Correlated Subquery
The inner query runs once independently.
Its result is used by the outer query
Example:
SELECT name, salary
FROM employees
WHERE salary > (
    SELECT AVG(salary)
    FROM employees
);

Correlated Subquery
The inner query depends on the outer query.
It runs once for every row of the outer query.
Example:
SELECT e1.name, e1.salary
FROM employees e1
WHERE e1.salary > (
    SELECT AVG(e2.salary)
    FROM employees e2
    WHERE e2.department = e1.department
);

| Feature     | Non-Correlated Subquery                           | Correlated Subquery                                        |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| Execution   | Inner query runs **once**                         | Inner query runs **for every row** of outer query          |
| Dependency  | Independent                                       | Depends on outer query’s column                            |
| Performance | Faster                                            | Slower                                                     |
| Example use | Compare to a single value (e.g., overall average) | Compare to group-specific value (e.g., department average) |

Why do we use GROUP BY before HAVING in SQL?
Because HAVING filters groups, not rows —
and groups don’t exist until after GROUP BY has been executed.
So you must first form groups (GROUP BY)
👉 then filter them (HAVING).



